+/*
+ * WGSE: libm-independent log10/log10f/pow/log1p for the Windows on Arm GKL build.
+ *
+ * GKL's PairHMM uses log10/log10f for its context tables and every final result,
+ * and pow/log1p for its jacobian and match-to-match tables. None of them is
+ * correctly rounded, so each C runtime returns its own last-bit answers: the
+ * Windows Arm64 UCRT differs from glibc on ~0.2% of PairHMM results and on some
+ * table entries. On _WIN32 && __aarch64__ these functions instead reproduce glibc
+ * 2.39's aarch64 libm bit for bit (the Linux reference): fdlibm's log10, log10f and
+ * log1p (Copyright (C) 1993 by Sun Microsystems, Inc., freely distributable with
+ * this notice) and Arm optimized-routines log, logf and pow (Copyright (c) 2017-2025
+ * Arm Limited, SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT OR Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception),
+ * with the fused multiply-adds GCC emits for glibc on AArch64 written as explicit
+ * fma calls. The tables are optimized-routines' __log_data, __logf_data,
+ * __pow_log_data and __exp_data, as configured in glibc. powf is not replaced: GKL
+ * only evaluates it on 128 fixed inputs, where the UCRT and glibc agree.
+ * They are not inlined, so the build's no-FMA guard can exempt exactly these functions.
+ * Elsewhere these forward to the platform libm, so other builds are unchanged.
+ */
